  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES: \n\n- Showers and thunderstorms today with frequent\n  lightning, brief heavy rain, and erratic gusts. \n\n- Elevated post-frontal winds creating choppy conditions on \n  Flathead Lake through early Sunday. \n\n- Unseasonably cold temperatures (10 to 15 degrees below \n  average) bringing high- elevation valley frost Sunday and Monday\n  mornings. \n\n- Brief warm-up early next week followed by another \n  cool, active trough late next week.\n\nToday-Sunday:\nNumerous showers and thunderstorms have developed across north-\ncentral Idaho into northwest Montana ahead of an approaching cold\nfront currently positioned along the Idaho/Washington border. This\nfront will push steadily across the region through this afternoon\nand evening. Storms along and ahead of the boundary will be\ncapable of brief moderate to heavy rainfall, lightning, and gusty,\nerratic outflow winds.  \n\nBehind the frontal passage, surface winds will become widespread\nand remain elevated through the overnight hours into Sunday. These\ngusty winds will generate choppy, hazardous boating conditions on\nFlathead Lake early Sunday morning before diminishing in the\nafternoon. Meanwhile, the main center of the low pressure system\nwill track along the Canadian border tonight into Sunday, keeping\nshowers  and isolated thunderstorms lingering across northwest \nMontana.\n\nThis unseasonably cold airmass will push temperatures roughly 10\nto 15 degrees below average for late August. Clearer skies and\ncold air drainage will allow morning low temperatures to drop into\nthe upper 20s to low 30s across the high-elevation valleys of\nnorth-central Idaho and western Montana on both Sunday and Monday\nmornings, resulting in widespread frost. \n\nMonday-Late Next Week:\nA transient ridge of high pressure will build over the region\nMonday into Tuesday, returning warm and dry conditions to the\nNorthern Rockies. However, long-range model guidance remains in\nstrong agreement that another significant trough of low pressure\nwill dig into the Pacific Northwest during the latter part of next\nweek, ushering in another stretch of cooler temperatures and\nactive weather.",
